Title: Turn scanner allocation failures into compilation errors instead of exit(2)
Body:
## Problem

`pcap_compile()` on a filter with a Flex scanner internal failure — most easily triggered by an allocation failure while the scanner grows its buffer stack — prints `out of dynamic memory in yy_scan_bytes()` and **terminates the whole process with `exit(2)`**. A library must not do this; any multithreaded or long-running application using libpcap dies with it.

Reproducer (before this PR): fail the Nth `malloc()` during `pcap_compile("tcp port 80")` and the process exits with status 2 instead of returning an error. I found this with an allocation-failure sweep over `pcap_compile()` (every allocation failed in turn, 8 filter corpora); 41 of the sweep runs terminated the process.

## Root cause

`scanner.l` leaves `YY_FATAL_ERROR` at the Flex default, which prints to stderr and calls `exit(2)`, bypassing the compiler's normal error machinery (`bpf_set_error` + error returns).

The same sweep also exposed leaks on the scanner error paths:

1. `yy_scan_bytes()` `malloc()`s a copy of the filter string, then allocates the buffer state; if the second allocation fails, the fatal error path leaks the copy.
2. `yy_scan_buffer()` allocates the buffer state, then `yy_switch_to_buffer()` → `yyensure_buffer_stack()` may allocate the buffer *stack*; if that fails, the just-allocated buffer state is unreachable and leaks.
3. An aborted scan leaked the `getaddrinfo()` results in `cstate.ai`.

## Fix

- Define `YY_FATAL_ERROR` in `scanner.l` in terms of a new `scanner_fatal_error()` (gencode.c), which reports through `bpf_set_error()` and `longjmp()`s to a dedicated `scan_ctx` jump target (a separate target from `top_ctx`, which code generator routines replace with their own `setjmp()`s while they run). `pcap_compile()` treats it as an ordinary compilation failure: `PCAP_ERROR` with the message in `errbuf`, and full cleanup.
- Make `pcap_compile()` own the scanner input buffer (`pcap__scan_buffer()` with two trailing NULs) instead of using `pcap__scan_string()`, so the leak in `yy_scan_bytes()` cannot occur.
- Pre-allocate the scanner buffer stack (`pcapint_prime_buffer_stack()`, two slots) *before* any buffer state exists, so nothing can fail between allocating a buffer state and installing it. This closes the `yy_scan_buffer()` leak without touching generated code.
- Free `cstate.ai` on the abort path.

## Test

`testprogs/oomtest.c` (new) defines `malloc()`/`calloc()`/`realloc()`/`free()` over a private arena (link-time interposition against `libpcap_static`, no special build options) and, for 7 filter expressions × both optimisation settings, fails **every** allocation of `pcap_compile()` in turn. For each it asserts that the process survives, that the call either succeeds or returns `PCAP_ERROR` with an error message, and that no memory is left behind.

Wired into `TESTrun` (`accept_oom_compile`) and ctest; also builds with autotools.

Verified against the unpatched library: the test fails immediately (`exit(2)` from Flex). With the patch: `TESTrun` 21,572 tests — 15,003 passed, 6,569 skipped, **0 failed** (including the new test), and an additional fault-injection sweep of 1,420 runs is clean.

The allocation-failure sweep also audited the pcapng size arithmetic (interface-array growth, option-length/padding math) and found it already well-guarded.
Files (7): gencode.c, gencode.h, scanner.l, testprogs/CMakeLists.txt, testprogs/Makefile.in, testprogs/TESTrun, testprogs/oomtest.c
